# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reference figure for genre `bitfield` — NORMATIVE surface only (spec/genres/bitfield.md).
# Every construct here is inside the v0.1 conformance surface and the
# compatibility promise. This genre reaches NO experimental construct at all
# `stroke=` was promoted to NORMATIVE (`STROKE-KEY-STATUS`) and `color=`
# was retired (`COLOUR-KEY-STATUS`), which were the only two it could ever reach, so
# bitfield-experimental.fd was deleted rather than left empty.
# The set demonstrates every FORM of every keyword, not just every keyword.
# Coverage: node tools/reference-coverage.js --normative-only --strict bitfield
figdown 0.1 bitfield
title "Telemetry export framing"

# `class` declares a meaning ONCE; elements join it with class=. The meaning
# string is required — it is what a reading agent gets instead of a hex value.
# `style=` is NOT among them here: it cannot reach a field by ANY route.
# It left `field`'s own option list (`STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`), and a class that
# declares it is silently ignored on a field member while the legend still
# draws the dash — a swatch for ink no field can carry. `tools/legend-check.js`
# is the gate for that; this class uses `stroke=`, which does reach.
class vendor   "Vendor-defined — not interoperable"  stroke=#7c3aed
class present  "Present only when the C flag is set" fill=#fef3c7
class reserved "Reserved — senders write zero"       fill=#e7e5e4

# `numbering=` is REQUIRED and has NO default (`UNSAFE-DEFAULT-ELIMINATION`). msb0 is the IETF packet
# convention: bit 0 is the MSB, ruler 0…N-1 left to right. A defaulted
# direction would render a ruler asserting something false, and a wrong ruler
# looks exactly like a right one. `word=` is the row width in bits; 32 is also
# the default, written here so the reader need not know that to read the rows.
bitfield hdr "Export header (on the wire)" word=32 numbering=msb0
# Classic form. The name is a WHITESPACE-delimited position, so `QUOTING-RULES` requires
# quotes even when the name contains no space: `field Ver 4` is a line error.
field "Ver" 4
# `description=` is authored documentation prose (IEEE 1685's spelling; it was
# `note=` until 0.1). It is in the MODEL but its only ink is an SVG
# <title> tooltip, so a fact a human must SEE belongs in the label or in a
# class meaning instead. §12.7 lets an agent quote or display it, never parse it.
field "Header Length" 6 description="in 32-bit words; minimum 3"
field "Flags" 6 class=vendor
# `present=` is "present only if", not "variable width": the width is fixed and
# only the presence is not, so a reader computing offsets ACROSS it must branch
# and emit both offset sets (`BITFIELD-CONDITIONAL-OFFSETS`). The renderer dashes it, and a STATED
# condition also draws a caption under the block. Both written forms are here:
# `present="…"` (condition stated) on Checksum, `present=""` (conditional,
# condition NOT stated — the author claimed nothing) on Sequence.
field "Checksum" 16 present="the C flag is set" class=present
# The dash IS `present=` and nothing can clear it: `style=` left `field`
# entirely (`STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`) because `style=solid` used to erase the dash
# while the model still recorded the field as conditionally present — one line,
# two different figures for a human and for a reading agent (`PRESENTATION-AS-MEANING-CARRIER`).
field "Sequence" 32 present=""
# Wider than `word=`: the field spans the following rows automatically and is
# still ONE field — one box, name written once. Splitting it into per-row
# pieces would assert two fields on the wire that do not exist.
field "Export Timestamp" 64 fill=#dbeafe
# class= takes a comma-separated LIST. The channels apply left to right and
# the later class wins per channel; here they touch different channels, so the
# fill comes from `reserved` and the outline colour from `vendor`.
field "Pad" 8 class=reserved,vendor
# `break` ends the drawing row explicitly. It is presentation only: it adds,
# skips and reorders no bits, and the blank cells it leaves are NOT bits. It
# needs a field declared since the block opened or since the previous break.
break
# `*` fills the remainder of the current row — the variable-length tail. At
# most one per block, and the budget is per BLOCK, so `tlv` below has its own.
field "Records" * description="record count derived from Header Length"

# The `Records` region above, expanded — one record, in a block of its own
# with its own ruler; the same device examples/srh.fd uses for the SRH's
# Flags octet. This block is where `index=` lives (`BITFIELD-REPETITION-CONSTRUCT`), and it
# needs a repeated structure to live in: the key says a `field` is ONE
# ELEMENT of a repeated run, and before it existed a run had to be written as
# N literal `field` lines, which put N objects in the model and so ASSERTED a
# cardinality the wire does not have. All three written forms are below, and
# they are a tri-state with the same shape as `present=`'s: an ABSENT key
# claims nothing at all — it is NOT a claim that the field occurs once —
# `index=""` claims repetition and states no index, and a range states one.
bitfield rec "Export record (one entry of the Records region)" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "Entry Count" 16 description="flow entries in this record; the entries are numbered from 1"
# DETERMINATE: both ends are literal integers, so the run is |last-first|+1 =
# 8 elements and the count is IN the document. Quotes are inert here (RULE
# 2.3) — `index="0..7"` says exactly what this line says, because determinacy
# is decided by parsing both ends and never by quoting. Descent needs no key
# of its own: `index=7..0` is the same eight gauges counted the other way.
field "Queue Depth" 16 index=0..7
# …and that is what a determinate run buys: this field's offset is still
# computable — 16 + 8×16 = 144 bits in — even though only TWO of the eight
# elements above are DRAWN (the first and the last, `REPEATED-RUN-DRAWING`). The elision strip
# between them occupies NO bit positions, so a
# reader who counts drawn cells is reading the drawing (`MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE`) and will get
# this offset wrong; the count comes from `index=`, never from the picture.
field "Drop Count" 32
# PROSE last index: the count is in `Entry Count` on the wire and NOT in this
# document, so the run is indeterminate and so is every offset after it — but
# that is now reached from SYNTAX rather than from a reader recognising a
# label. The prose is never parsed or resolved: an agent may quote and
# display "Entry Count" and MUST NOT match it to the field of that name
# (§12.7, the locator problem). `<first>` is ALWAYS a literal integer, and
# that is what lets a 1-based run be drawn 1-based — the canonical form is
# 0-based, from the C indexing semantics of the IEEE 1685 element this key is
# borrowed from, but a format that numbers its entries from 1 says 1 rather
# than being renumbered into a convention it does not follow.
field "Flow Entry" 64 index="1..Entry Count"
# `index=""`: it repeats, and nothing whatever about the indices is stated.
# ONE occurrence draws, with a bare elision mark under it and no index on
# either — the same ruling `present=""` gets for its caption. Two boxes would
# assert a first and a last the author declined to state, and an invented
# `[0] … [n-1]` would be the engine speaking for them.
field "Vendor Counter" 32 index="" class=vendor

# No label written: the model records it ABSENT and the renderer falls back to
# the id (`OMITTED-LABEL-RECORDING`) — never invent a caption. No `word=` either, so the row is the
# default 32 bits.
bitfield tlv numbering=msb0
# Compact form (the C bit-field convention): ONE whitespace-free token, items
# comma-delimited. A name stays BARE here unless it contains whitespace, a
# comma, a quote, a paren or `#` — the exact inverse of the classic form's
# rule, because a comma-delimited element needs no quotes to terminate.
field type:8,length:8,flags:8,rsvd:8
# A compact name containing SPACES must be quoted: the list terminates at the
# first whitespace, so an unquoted spaced name cannot be told from the next
# item. A name containing a COMMA cannot be written compactly at all.
field "Scope Count":16,"Element Count":16
# Compact options are LINE-WIDE — one class covers all four items, exactly as
# the classic form's single option set applies to its one field. All four
# inherit `vendor`'s outline colour. Note what a class can NOT hand a field:
# a DASH. Since `style=` left `field` (`STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`), `present=` is the
# only source of one, and a class declaring `style=` reaches the legend and
# nothing else. Line-wide is also why `index=` is NOT available here (`BITFIELD-REPETITION-CONSTRUCT`):
# a line-wide range would say all four items repeat over the SAME indices,
# which is not what any of them says. A repeated element is written in the
# classic form, on a line of its own — `rec` above.
field e1:8,e2:8,e3:8,e4:8 class=vendor
field body:*   # `*` is available in the compact form too

# lsb0 is the hardware-register convention: bit 0 is the LSB, so the ruler
# runs N-1…0 left to right. `numbering=` relabels the RULER and nothing else —
# placement is left to right in declaration order under either value — so a
# register is written from its high end down, the way a datasheet prints it.
# `fill=` and `stroke=` on the block are the DEFAULT cell fill and border; a
# field's own fill=/stroke= wins. `stroke=` is NORMATIVE
# (`STROKE-KEY-STATUS`) — an outline on a shape, and the whole line on an edge/bundle/
# threshold, which is SVG's own asymmetry borrowed unaltered.
bitfield ctl "Exporter control register (0x40)" word=16 numbering=lsb0 fill=#fafaf9 stroke=#a8a29e
# Option position is free on every directive (`OPTION-POSITION-PARSING`) — before the name or after.
field class=reserved "RSVD" 4
field "MODE" 3 stroke=#b45309   # a field's own stroke= beats the block's
field "INTERVAL" 8 fill=#dbeafe
field "EN" 1 description="1 = exporter enabled"
